# What should you play as White?
teaches: ["openings/italian-game"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "What is the idea behind each move of the Italian Game? What is White's plan with c3 and d4?"
next: {"title":"What should Black play against 1.e4?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/16-what-should-black-play-against-1-e4.cast"}

## Warm-up: back rank and combinations
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 3r2k1/5ppp/1q6/8/8/8/4QPPP/4R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Qe8+, Rxe8, Rxe8#]}
      - {fen: 3q1rk1/pp3ppp/8/8/8/3B4/PPPR1PPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxh7+, Kxh7, Rxd8, Rxd8, Rxd8]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today's question: what should you play as White? First, two warm-up puzzles from last week.
    draw position

B: Material's level. I'd trade queens and call it a draw.
    highlight piece_e2 piece_b6

A: Before you offer a draw, run the Three Questions: checks first.
    highlight piece_f7 piece_g7 piece_h7

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in two." widget chess line true hints ["A check, even if it costs your queen.","After the recapture, the rook on e1 is still there."] themes ["back rank"] voice a teaches ["safety/back-rank"]
        right: Queen to e8, check. The rook on d8 takes her, and the rook on e1 takes back: mate, with Black's own pawns locking the king in.
        wrong: Checks first: which check is there, and what comes after the recapture?

B: I gave the queen away, and it was the best move on the board.
    point at.ref piece_g8 gesture circle

A: Second one. Black's queen is in line with your bishop and rooks on the d-file.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Any bishop move uncovers my rook on her. But she just steps away.
    highlight piece_d3 piece_d8

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A bishop move she has no time to answer.","Which bishop move gives check?"] themes ["discovered attack","combination"] voice a teaches ["tactics/discovered-attack","calculation/combination"]
        right: Bishop takes h7 with check, so the queen gets no time. The king takes, your rook takes the queen, the rooks trade on d8, and your rook is left.
        wrong: A bishop move with check would leave her no time to step away.

B: A bishop for a queen.
    point at.ref sq_h7 gesture circle

A: Forcing moves won both. Today: the moves before any tactic, your opening as White.
    highlight piece_d8

## The Italian, move by move
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5]}
      - {fen: r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/2b1p3/2B1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 4 4, moves: [Ng5]}
      - {fen: r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/2b1p1N1/2B1P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 5 4, moves: [Qxg5], flip: true}
      - {fen: r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/2b1p3/2B1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 4 4, moves: [c3, Nf6, d4, exd4, cxd4, Bb4+, Bd2], arrows: [{from: c3, to: d4}], highlights: [d4, e4]}
      - {fen: r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/2n2n2/2b1p3/2B1P3/2P2N2/PP1P1PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 1 5, moves: [d3, d6, O-O, O-O, Re1], arrows: [{from: d3, to: d4}], highlights: [e4]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    highlights:
      - f7
sources:
    - id: italian
      title: Italian Game
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 is one of the oldest recorded chess openings; the bishop targets f7, a square defended only by the king.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Italian_Game
    - id: piano
      title: Giuoco Piano
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: Giuoco Piano is Italian for 'quiet game'; c3 prepares d4 so White gets the centre without spending two tempi on the d-pawn; Greco developed 4.c3 Nf6 5.d4 exd4 6.cxd4 Bb4+ in the early 17th century; 7.Bd2 is White's most popular reply; 5.d3 (Giuoco Pianissimo) became more common than 5.d4 and the c3+d3 lines see frequent high-level play.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giuoco_Piano

    card title "The Italian Game"

A: Now the opening.
    draw position

A: Pawn to e4 takes a centre square and frees the queen and the bishop.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: And Black copies.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: Knight to f3 develops, and attacks the pawn on e5.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

B: So Black guards it with a knight.
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.3

A: Bishop to c4 aims at f7,
    animate plies_shown 5 duration 1.3

A: the one square only Black's king guards.
    point at.ref piece_f7 gesture circle

A: Black mirrors it, aiming at f2.
    animate plies_shown 6 duration 1.3

A: This is the Italian Game, one of the oldest openings on record.
    highlight piece_c4 piece_c5

    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.3

B: Then I hit f7 again. Knight to g5!
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: Two attackers on f7.
    highlight piece_g5 piece_c4

A: Now look from Black's side: the board turns.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Punish it." widget chess line true hints ["The Three Questions: any captures?","Your queen's diagonal is open."] themes ["hanging piece"] voice a teaches ["safety/hanging-pieces"]
        right: Queen takes g5: her diagonal was open, and nothing guarded the knight.
        wrong: Which black piece can capture the knight on g5?

B: The knight just hung. The Three Questions would have caught it.
    point at.ref sq_g5 gesture circle

A: Back to White's side.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

A: The masters' move is quiet: pawn to c3.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: It prepares d4, so a pawn can take back there.
    draw arrow_0

    erase arrow_0

B: Black's knight attacks e4.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: Now d4: the pawn hits e5 and the bishop at once.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

A: Black takes.
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.3

A: And the c-pawn takes back.
    animate plies_shown 5 duration 1.3

A: Two pawns side by side, and the bishop is attacked again.
    draw hl_d4 hl_e4 parallel true

    erase hl_d4 hl_e4

B: Bishop check!
    animate plies_shown 6 duration 1.3

A: Block it with your bishop, and offer a trade.
    animate plies_shown 7 duration 1.3

A: This is the Giuoco Piano, Italian for quiet game. Gioachino Greco played c3 and d4 here around 1620.
    highlight piece_d4 piece_e4

B: Quiet? Pawns everywhere, and my e4 pawn is loose.
    highlight piece_e4

A: Black's knight hits e4. Which guard costs least, and keeps the queen home?
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 box.x 20 box.y 100 box.w 600 box.h 570 duration 1

    quiz "Black's knight hits e4. Which guard costs least, and keeps the queen home?" on_canvas true say_question false buttons_at.x 810 buttons_at.y 420 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: Pawn to d3: a pawn guards e4, the queen stays home, and c3 still backs up d4 later.
        wrong: Compare what each guard costs, and where the queen ends up.
        + d3
        * Qe2
        * Ng5

A: Pawn to d3: the Giuoco Pianissimo, the very quiet game.
    animate plies_shown 1 box.x 158 box.y 20 box.w 684 box.h 684 duration 1.3

A: Many strong players choose it today.
    highlight piece_d3 piece_e4

A: Castle,
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.6

A: bring a rook to e1,
    animate plies_shown 5 duration 1.6

A: and push d4 when it's ready.
    draw arrow_0

A: Fast or slow, one plan: c3, then d4, a big centre behind safe pieces.
    highlight piece_c3 piece_d3 piece_e4

## Your turn: play the Italian
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r2qk1nr/ppp2ppp/1bnpb3/8/2BPP3/2N2N2/PP3PPP/R1BQK2R w KQkq - 3 8, moves: [d5, Bd7, dxc6]}
      - {fen: r1b1k1nr/ppp2ppp/2np4/2b5/2B1q3/2P2N2/PP1P1PPP/RNBQ1RK1 w kq - 0 1, moves: [Re1, Qe7, Rxe7+, Ngxe7]}
      - {fen: r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/1bn5/4P3/2B1n3/2P2N2/PP3PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 0 7, moves: [Qd5, Bxf2+, Kf1, O-O, Qxe4]}
      - {fen: r3k2r/pp2bppp/2n3q1/3N4/8/4B3/PPP2PPP/R2Q1RK1 w kq - 0 1, moves: [Nc7+, Kf8, Nxa8]}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5, c3, Nf6, d4]}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5, c3, Nf6, d3]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    openings:
      - {name: Italian Game, eco: C50, side: white, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4], idea: 'The bishop aims at f7, the square only Black''s king guards.'}
      - {name: 'Giuoco Piano, with d4', eco: C54, side: white, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5, c3, Nf6, d4], idea: 'c3 came first, so a pawn can take back on d4: a big centre.'}
      - {name: 'Giuoco Pianissimo, with d3', eco: C54, side: white, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5, c3, Nf6, d3], idea: 'A pawn guards e4; castle first, and push d4 later.'}
sources:
    - id: piano
      title: Giuoco Piano
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: 4.c3 prepares d4 (Giuoco Piano, 5.d4) or the slower 5.d3 (Giuoco Pianissimo).
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giuoco_Piano

    card title "Your turn"

A: First: four puzzles, three on Italian themes and one from week two. Then the moves themselves.
    draw position

A: Black's bishop just came to e6, beside the knight.
    highlight piece_e6 piece_c6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win a piece." widget chess line true hints ["A pawn can attack two pieces.","The pawn on d4."] themes ["fork","opening"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork","openings/italian-game"]
        right: Pawn to d5 forks the knight and the bishop. The bishop steps back to d7, and the pawn takes the knight: however Black answers, a piece falls.
        wrong: Which of your pawns can attack two black pieces at once?

B: The d4 pawn from the plan did that.
    highlight piece_c6

A: Next, Black's queen has grabbed a pawn on e4.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Black's king and queen share a file.","A rook to the e-file."] themes ["pin","opening"] voice a teaches ["tactics/pin","openings/italian-game"]
        right: Rook to e1 pins the queen to her king. She can only move along the file, and every square there, even taking the rook, loses her for a rook. Here she steps to e7, and the rook takes her with check.
        wrong: Look at the e-file: what stands behind Black's queen?

A: Here it's a knight that took on e4.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line 1 hints ["Two targets: a loose knight, and f7.","One queen move hits both."] themes ["fork","opening"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork","openings/italian-game"]
        right: Queen to d5: she threatens mate on f7 and attacks the knight on e4, both at once.
        wrong: Find one queen move that hits f7 and the knight together.

A: But Black checks first.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.2

A: The king steps aside.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.2

A: Black castles out of the mate.
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.2

A: And the knight falls.
    animate plies_shown 5 duration 1.2

A: One from week two: Black's king is still in the middle.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A knight check.","Which square touches the king and the rook?"] themes ["fork"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork"]
        right: Knight to c7, check, forks the king and the rook. The king steps aside, and the knight takes the rook on a8.
        wrong: Which knight move gives check and hits something big?

B: That's why I should castle early.
    point at.ref sq_e8 gesture circle

A: Now the lines themselves: the big centre with d4,
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 9 duration 0.6

A: and the quiet one with d3.
    animate position_shown 5 plies_shown 9 duration 0.6

A: The drill opens on the board: play them from memory, as White.
    explore activity openings_drill

A: Every Italian move has a job:
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 9 duration 0.6

A: the centre, a new piece, f7, and c3 so that d4 holds.
    highlight piece_e4 piece_f3 piece_c4 piece_c3

A: Know the job, and you'll find the move when the book runs out.
    highlight piece_d4

    erase position

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower.
    text hw "15 + 10" x 500 y 500 font_size 110

A: Then find its first mistake.
    text hw2 "1st mistake?" x 500 y 300 font_size 84

